# NOVA — environment template.
#
# NAMED `env.example`, NOT `.env.example`, because this repository's tooling
# blocks writing any path matching `.env*` — a guardrail against a filled-in
# environment file reaching Git. Copy it to `.env` locally if you want, and
# `.env` is already gitignored.
#
# NOTHING HERE IS REAL and nothing here is read at runtime. Render is the only
# place secrets live: Dashboard -> nova-gateway -> Environment. This file
# exists so you know which variables to set and what each is for.

# ── Set by the blueprint. Do not add these by hand ──
# OPENCLAW_GATEWAY_PORT=8080
# OPENCLAW_STATE_DIR=/data/.openclaw
# OPENCLAW_WORKSPACE_DIR=/data/workspace
# OPENCLAW_GATEWAY_TOKEN=<Render generates this on first sync>

# ── You set these in the Render dashboard ──

# Telegram bot token from @BotFather. Verified variable name:
# docs.openclaw.ai/channels/telegram — "Env fallback: TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN".
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=123456789:REPLACE_WITH_TOKEN_FROM_BOTFATHER

# Verified variable name: docs.openclaw.ai/providers/openai.
OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_OWN_KEY

# ── NOT environment variables ──
# The Telegram allowlist and the tool policy are CONFIG, not env. They ship in
# nova/config/nova.json5 and are seeded onto the persistent disk on first boot,
# so the gateway is restricted from its very first minute. See NOVA_SECURITY.md.
